1
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:14,000
Last time we met the four steps of CALL. This time, let's follow one real program all the way through.

2
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:20,933
First, a quick check: after which step are all the machine code bits of these two instructions known?

3
00:00:20,933 --> 00:00:26,466
Step one: the compiler translates the C code into assembly, hello.s.

4
00:00:26,466 --> 00:00:33,766
Note that la, call, li and ret are pseudo-instructions, left for the assembler to expand.

5
00:00:33,766 --> 00:00:41,566
Lines starting with a dot are directives: they produce no machine instructions, they tell the assembler how to lay out the object file.

6
00:00:41,566 --> 00:00:49,033
.text says what follows goes in the text segment; .align 2 aligns it to 2² = 4 bytes.

7
00:00:49,033 --> 00:00:55,400
.global (also spelled .globl) makes main a global symbol that other files can refer to.

8
00:00:55,400 --> 00:01:01,466
Then we switch to the read-only data section, .rodata, again aligned to 4 bytes.

9
00:01:01,466 --> 00:01:09,100
.string stores a null-terminated string; the labels str1 and str2 mark where each one starts.

10
00:01:09,100 --> 00:01:18,700
Two other common directives aren't used here: .data switches to the data segment, and .word stores 32-bit words one after another.

11
00:01:18,700 --> 00:01:27,433
Step two: the assembler produces the object file hello.o. It's binary: looked at directly, it's just a run of hex numbers.

12
00:01:27,433 --> 00:01:34,333
Disassembling main: addresses within the module on the left, machine code in the middle, instructions on the right.

13
00:01:34,333 --> 00:01:42,666
The pseudo-instructions are expanded. This listing is simplified: call became a single jalr.

14
00:01:42,666 --> 00:01:50,400
But these five have immediates of 0, just placeholders: the assembler doesn't yet know where the data or printf will be.

15
00:01:50,400 --> 00:01:56,233
The other instructions involve no addresses, so their machine code is final after assembly.

16
00:01:56,233 --> 00:02:03,533
The symbol table lists this file's labels and their offsets within their segments; the debugger gdb uses it too.

17
00:02:03,533 --> 00:02:09,133
main sits at offset 0 of the text segment and is global: that's what .global did.

18
00:02:09,133 --> 00:02:17,600
Both strings are local data. The first is 12 bytes including its terminating 0, so the second starts at 0xc.

19
00:02:17,600 --> 00:02:22,733
The relocation table is the linker's to-do list: one entry per placeholder.

20
00:02:22,733 --> 00:02:31,466
The first two: the lui at 0x8 waits for the upper 20 bits of str1's address, the addi at 0xc for the lower 12.

21
00:02:31,466 --> 00:02:40,066
Same for str2; the last entry, at 0x18, is the jalr waiting for printf's address.

22
00:02:40,066 --> 00:02:45,433
Why can the assembler settle some addresses but not others? Because there are three kinds.

23
00:02:45,433 --> 00:02:55,533
One: PC-relative jumps within the file, like beq, or jal to a local label. Wherever the code moves, the distance stays the same.

24
00:02:55,533 --> 00:03:05,233
Two: addresses of static data, like str1 for la, or a global that lw reads. They're known only once all the files are put together.

25
00:03:05,233 --> 00:03:13,233
Three: external functions like printf. It lives in another file, so the assembler can't even tell how far away it is.

26
00:03:13,233 --> 00:03:23,100
The last two go into the relocation table. So jal sometimes needs editing, but B-type branches stay within the module and never do.

27
00:03:23,100 --> 00:03:31,200
Step three: the linker combines hello.o, the start-up code and printf from the library into the executable a.out.

28
00:03:31,200 --> 00:03:40,300
Where's printf? The linker searches the user's .o files first, then the libraries. A .a library is just a bundle of .o files.

29
00:03:40,300 --> 00:03:48,466
Linking also makes separate compilation possible: change hello.c and only it gets recompiled, not the huge C library.

30
00:03:48,466 --> 00:03:57,866
First the text segments are laid end to end from 0x10000: start-up code first, then main, and further on, printf.

31
00:03:57,866 --> 00:04:06,300
The data segment follows the code: str1 lands at 0x20A10, str2 12 bytes later.

32
00:04:06,300 --> 00:04:11,300
Every symbol now has its final address, and the linker updates the symbol table.

33
00:04:11,300 --> 00:04:18,166
Then it works through the relocation table, filling each hole. First, every instruction gets its final address.

34
00:04:18,166 --> 00:04:31,133
Start with str1 = 0x20A10. The low 12 bits, 0xA10, have their top bit set, so addi reads them as the negative number -1520.

35
00:04:31,133 --> 00:04:38,333
So the upper 20 bits get an extra 1: 0x21. It's the same split li uses, from Episode 12.

36
00:04:38,333 --> 00:04:45,600
0x21 goes into lui's immediate field: the placeholder zeros become real address bits.

37
00:04:45,600 --> 00:04:49,600
addi gets -1520, and both instructions are complete.

38
00:04:49,600 --> 00:04:56,166
Likewise for str2: lui gets 0x21 again, addi gets -1508.

39
00:04:56,166 --> 00:05:03,200
Finally, printf: it's at 0x10450, 0x288 bytes from the call.

40
00:05:03,200 --> 00:05:11,433
jal reaches ±1 MiB, which is plenty, so the linker rewrites this as a single jal.

41
00:05:11,433 --> 00:05:20,033
The other instructions are untouched. Add a header and debugging info, and a.out is done: every bit is now fixed.

42
00:05:20,033 --> 00:05:25,133
Back to the quick check: add involves no addresses, so it's settled after assembly.

43
00:05:25,133 --> 00:05:33,266
But jal targets fprintf, an external function in the stdio library, so it's settled only after linking.

44
00:05:33,266 --> 00:05:41,233
What we just did is static linking: the library code is copied into a.out. The program is self-contained, but large.

45
00:05:41,233 --> 00:05:48,066
When the library fixes a bug or a security hole, every program must be relinked and shipped again.

46
00:05:48,066 --> 00:05:55,333
Dynamic linking keeps the library in its own file, such as libc.so, and links it in when the program is loaded.

47
00:05:55,333 --> 00:06:03,366
Programs get smaller, and they can share one copy of the library in memory. Replace libc.so and they all upgrade.

48
00:06:03,366 --> 00:06:11,633
The cost: linking overhead at run time, and a.out alone no longer runs. On balance, it's still worth it.

49
00:06:11,633 --> 00:06:19,700
A bonus, not tested: instead of translating a program, we can hand it to an interpreter, another program that runs it directly.

50
00:06:19,700 --> 00:06:29,600
Python is fully interpreted: easiest to write, slowest to run. Java compiles to bytecode, which is then interpreted, so it runs anywhere.

51
00:06:29,600 --> 00:06:39,133
C compiles to machine code and is much faster. Machine code is the hardest to write, yet the easiest to "interpret": the hardware runs it directly.

52
00:06:39,133 --> 00:06:46,933
Machine code can be interpreted in software too: Venus simulates RISC-V one instruction at a time, so you can step through it.

53
00:06:46,933 --> 00:06:53,733
Each time Apple switched ISAs, software ran the programs built for the old one. The latest is Rosetta.

54
00:06:53,733 --> 00:07:03,800
Interpreters are easier to write, debug and port; translated programs usually run 10 times faster or more, and keep the source private.

55
00:07:03,800 --> 00:07:12,666
Putting it together: compile to assembly, assemble to machine code with placeholders, fill in the addresses when linking, then load and run.

56
00:07:12,666 --> 00:07:19,900
The loader reads a.out into memory, the start-up code calls main, and Hello, world! appears.

57
00:07:19,900 --> 00:07:27,733
It's the whole series in miniature: registers, memory, branches, functions, instruction encoding, and CALL.

58
00:07:27,733 --> 00:07:35,066
From one line of printf to a string of settled bits: that's the road a program travels before it runs.

